  "worked_solution": "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-yz\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14234808/assets/9a59198b60398e2b3044296c3cc76b4748785303029dccc1087320cd11cb7d55.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{z}\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le z\\le \\frac{4}{y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14234808/assets/9bc88aa4f0ad07d89ff5dd3c0de16208cb8bf47d774b557a7bf2140790c95291.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$",
